	abstract = {We provide more sample-efficient versions of some basic routines in quantum data analysis, along with simpler proofs. Particularly, we give a quantum "Threshold Search" algorithm that requires only \$O(({\textbackslash}log{\textasciicircum}2 m)/ε{\textasciicircum}2)\$ samples of a \$d\$-dimensional state \$ρ\$. That is, given observables \$0 {\textbackslash}le A\_1, A\_2, ..., A\_m {\textbackslash}le 1\$ such that \${\textbackslash}mathrm\{tr\}(ρA\_i) {\textbackslash}ge 1/2\$ for at least one \$i\$, the algorithm finds \$j\$ with \${\textbackslash}mathrm\{tr\}(ρA\_j) {\textbackslash}ge 1/2-ε\$. As a consequence, we obtain a Shadow Tomography algorithm requiring only \${\textbackslash}tilde\{O\}(({\textbackslash}log{\textasciicircum}2 m)({\textbackslash}log d)/ε{\textasciicircum}4)\$ samples, which simultaneously achieves the best known dependence on each parameter \$m\$, \$d\$, \$ε\$. This yields the same sample complexity for quantum Hypothesis Selection among \$m\$ states; we also give an alternative Hypothesis Selection method using \${\textbackslash}tilde\{O\}(({\textbackslash}log{\textasciicircum}3 m)/ε{\textasciicircum}2)\$ samples.},
