	url = {http://arxiv.org/abs/2305.19266},
	abstract = {We implement mid-circuit operations in a 48-site array of neutral atoms, enabled by new methods for control of the \${\textbackslash}textit\{omg\}\$ (optical-metastable-ground state qubit) architecture present in \$\{\}{\textasciicircum}\{171\}\$Yb. We demonstrate laser-based control of ground, metastable and optical qubits with average single-qubit fidelities of \$F\_\{g\} = 99.968(3)\$, \$F\_\{m\} = 99.12(4)\$ and \$F\_\{o\} = 99.804(8)\$. With state-sensitive shelving between the ground and metastable states, we realize a non-destructive state-detection for \${\textasciicircum}\{171\}\$Yb, and reinitialize in the ground state with either global control or local feed-forward operations. We use local addressing of the optical clock transition to perform mid-circuit operations, including measurement, spin reset, and motional reset in the form of ground-state cooling. In characterizing mid-circuit measurement on ground-state qubits, we observe raw errors of \$1.8(6){\textbackslash}\%\$ on ancilla qubits and \$4.5(1.0){\textbackslash}\%\$ on data qubits, with the former (latter) uncorrected for \$1.0(2){\textbackslash}\%\$ (\$2.0(2){\textbackslash}\%\$) preparation and measurement error; we observe similar performance for mid-circuit reset operations. The reported realization of the \${\textbackslash}textit\{omg\}\$ architecture and mid-circuit operations are door-opening for many tasks in quantum information science, including quantum error-correction, entanglement generation, and metrology.},
